WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.470 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  early morning gunfire echoes across the  stretch of the salwyn river along the  border between myanmar  and thailand this video from the thai  side appears to show korean national  union fighters  taking over a myanmar army outpost then  burning it to the ground  it's some of the most intense fighting  between ethnic armies and myanmar's  military since the coup  nearly three months ago the corrin  fighters are one of about two dozen  ethnic minority forces that have been  fighting the myanmar military for  decades  seeking greater autonomy like the korean  rebels many of them have thrown their  support behind the antiku protest  movement that has swept across myanmar  since the takeover  there has been talk about forming a  federal army  some feel the attack on the outpost was  a signal from the korean national union  to myanmar's army  locally known as the tapidon and this  shows that we have a plan in our area  to diminish your territory and take over  more of what we see as our territory  but also do it at a moment that  discredits the power of the tamil  um and which gives a symbolic boost i  think  uh to broader nationwide um uh  resistance against against the military  so it's it's it's a lot of different  things all wrapped up together  he went on to say that a strong  retaliation is expected  air strikes from the military on current  held territory have increased over the  last few weeks  at one point sending hundreds across the  border into thailand  seeking refuge and medical attention 24  000 people have been displaced since  late last month according to korean  groups  also on tuesday the military junta said  it will consider suggestions  on how to end the crisis in myanmar made  by the southeast asia regional bloc  asean in a saturday meeting the  announcement could be viewed as a  setback since leaders said a consensus  was already reached with ajunta on steps  to end the violence  some protesters are taking their battle  against the junta from the streets to  the jungle  this footage appears to show a training  camp run by ethnic armies  preparing those who want to transition  from protester to fighter  while there are still protesters  dedicated to taking their call for the  junta to step down  to the streets but in smaller numbers  because of the intensified crackdown by  security forces  making sure their voices are heard scott  heidler al jazeera  bangkok

